WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.602
This briefing comes from
Book 01 of the Federal

3
00:00:03.602 --> 00:00:06.587
Modernization Workbook,
The Transformation Arc.

4
00:00:06.587 --> 00:00:11.147
It's about the move from a domain-joined
client-server estate to cloud identity,

5
00:00:11.147 --> 00:00:14.513
cloud device management and
governed hybrid operations.

6
00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:18.540
Book 01 is the workbook's
overview: each part of it points to

7
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.400
the book that carries the detail,
so think of it as the map.

8
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.990
We'll take four questions:
why modernization is so hard,

9
00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:29.900
what actually has to change, who
leads it, and how to get there.

10
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.624
And if you remember one
thing, make it this:

11
00:00:32.624 --> 00:00:36.550
modernization is a governance
rebuild, not a platform swap.

12
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.040
Let's start with how the estate got here.

13
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.078
Why is it so hard?

14
00:00:44.078 --> 00:00:48.291
Because the estate was built in four
eras, and each one solved a real

15
00:00:48.291 --> 00:00:52.964
problem without keeping what the first
era had: one authoritative answer.

16
00:00:52.964 --> 00:00:57.340
On the mainframe, one machine held
the data and controlled access to it.

17
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.405
By the late 1980s, that
truth was spread across

18
00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:03.390
a dozen regional
databases and directories.

19
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:07.907
Then the direction reversed, and
data center consolidation wired

20
00:01:07.907 --> 00:01:12.177
offices back to distant facilities;
the slide has OMB's numbers.

21
00:01:12.177 --> 00:01:15.694
Finally, email and collaboration
moved to the cloud,

22
00:01:15.694 --> 00:01:19.128
while identity stayed
behind in regional domains.

23
00:01:19.128 --> 00:01:22.003
And each fix assumes the
last one is finished.

24
00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:27.407
Zero Trust assumes governed identity,
governed identity assumes a clean directory,

25
00:01:27.407 --> 00:01:30.341
and a clean directory
assumes someone owns it.

26
00:01:30.341 --> 00:01:32.380
Trust itself changed too.

27
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:37.905
Trust used to come from a session.

28
00:01:37.905 --> 00:01:40.674
In Active Directory, you
sign in once and get a

29
00:01:40.674 --> 00:01:43.668
Kerberos ticket that lasts
ten hours by default.

30
00:01:43.668 --> 00:01:47.793
An account disabled during the day
can keep using tickets it got earlier.

31
00:01:47.793 --> 00:01:51.618
Zero Trust decides every
request against current policy.

32
00:01:51.618 --> 00:01:54.506
An Entra access token
lasts an hour by default,

33
00:01:54.506 --> 00:01:59.599
or up to 28 hours with continuous access
evaluation, because the service listens

34
00:01:59.599 --> 00:02:04.692
for events like a disabled account and
stops honoring the token in near real time.

35
00:02:04.692 --> 00:02:07.218
Think of it as a badge
checked at every door.

36
00:02:07.218 --> 00:02:09.444
And distance doesn't break tickets,

37
00:02:09.444 --> 00:02:12.592
but it does slow every trip
to a domain controller.

38
00:02:12.592 --> 00:02:16.212
On the book's arithmetic,
Portland to Washington can't beat

39
00:02:16.212 --> 00:02:20.180
about 45 milliseconds round
trip, whatever the bandwidth.

40
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.505
Chapter 02 names the core problem.

41
00:02:26.505 --> 00:02:29.244
Active Directory was
the governance model for

42
00:02:29.244 --> 00:02:32.193
every Windows-era service,
and nobody wrote it down.

43
00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:37.234
Organizational units exist to apply
Group Policy and delegate authority,

44
00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:39.267
and both inherit down the tree.

45
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:44.104
So placing one user in an organizational
unit (OU) did five things at once:

46
00:02:44.104 --> 00:02:48.695
it chose their administrators,
applied every policy along the path,

47
00:02:48.695 --> 00:02:53.942
fed the groups built from position, set
delegation, and drew an audit boundary.

48
00:02:53.942 --> 00:02:56.217
Entra ID has no OU tree.

49
00:02:56.217 --> 00:02:58.321
Dynamic groups follow attributes,

50
00:02:58.321 --> 00:03:01.730
and administrative units scope
roles but can't be nested.

51
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.642
Neither rebuilds the tree on its own;
that has to be written down on purpose.

52
00:03:06.642 --> 00:03:09.659
And Microsoft still
supports Active Directory,

53
00:03:09.659 --> 00:03:13.790
so retiring it is the agency's
decision, not a vendor deadline.

54
00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.078
Most AD-to-Entra projects
are scoped around users,

55
00:03:21.078 --> 00:03:23.840
groups and mailboxes: the visible part.

56
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.414
But the program counts eleven categories
of object that Active Directory governs,

57
00:03:29.414 --> 00:03:34.665
from service accounts and Group Policy
to DNS, certificates, RADIUS and trusts.

58
00:03:34.665 --> 00:03:39.253
Migrate the users, declare victory,
and the rest gets left behind.

59
00:03:39.253 --> 00:03:41.427
Worse, those failures are quiet.

60
00:03:41.427 --> 00:03:45.628
A certificate doesn't renew, because
enrollment was never re-pointed.

61
00:03:45.628 --> 00:03:49.852
A device can't get on the network,
because a group lookup comes back empty.

62
00:03:49.852 --> 00:03:54.914
An LDAP application stops, because its
bind account was deleted with the domain.

63
00:03:54.914 --> 00:03:58.941
Projects fail because nobody
treats five steps as a discipline:

64
00:03:58.941 --> 00:04:04.183
enumerate everything, map each dependency
to a target, keep delegation continuous,

65
00:04:04.183 --> 00:04:08.120
deliver through a governed
pipeline, and watch for drift.

66
00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.158
Now, what changes.

67
00:04:13.158 --> 00:04:17.020
Before anything moves, the
program needs one place where

68
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:20.719
every decision is written
down, signed and replayable.

69
00:04:20.719 --> 00:04:24.399
Chapter 03 describes a
reference host for that:

70
00:04:24.399 --> 00:04:27.983
one Windows Server 2025
server, with a standby.

71
00:04:27.983 --> 00:04:32.433
It carries five roles, from a
version-controlled source of truth with signed

72
00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:36.883
commits to an adapter for each dependency
category; the slide lists them all.

73
00:04:36.883 --> 00:04:40.594
What matters most is that there's
a single authoritative writer.

74
00:04:40.594 --> 00:04:43.520
And governance and execution never mix.

75
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.670
One layer validates and signs
plans, but never touches a target.

76
00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:51.325
The other runs authorized
plans, but decides nothing,

77
00:04:51.325 --> 00:04:54.482
with separate accounts,
credentials and logs.

78
00:04:54.482 --> 00:04:59.760
The host reads Active Directory
read-only, and it's protected as Tier 0.

79
00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:08.322
Identity comes first, because every
later control reads its attributes.

80
00:05:08.322 --> 00:05:12.847
For the workforce, the authoritative
source is the HR system of record.

81
00:05:12.847 --> 00:05:17.787
Four mechanisms rebuild what the OU tree
did: attributes replace OU placement,

82
00:05:17.787 --> 00:05:23.184
dynamic groups replace OU-based groups,
administrative units replace OU delegation,

83
00:05:23.184 --> 00:05:26.984
and Conditional Access replaces
Group Policy inheritance.

84
00:05:26.984 --> 00:05:31.863
The program's contribution is what ties
them together: one HR-sourced stamp

85
00:05:31.863 --> 00:05:37.135
drives membership, delegation, policy
targeting, licensing and drift detection.

86
00:05:37.135 --> 00:05:38.298
Two cautions.

87
00:05:38.298 --> 00:05:41.719
Dynamic membership usually
updates within a few hours,

88
00:05:41.719 --> 00:05:45.810
but it can take more than a day, so
design job moves around that delay.

89
00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:50.248
And HR provisioning must be the only
thing that writes those attributes.

90
00:05:50.248 --> 00:05:54.900
Books 02 and 03 carry identity in depth.

91
00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.915
Here's why this matters: an Active
Directory forest holds four populations,

92
00:06:03.915 --> 00:06:08.500
not one, and each moves by its own
track, with its own source of truth.

93
00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:11.819
People are the HR track,
from the HR record or,

94
00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:14.862
for contractors, the contractor source.

95
00:06:14.862 --> 00:06:19.555
End-user devices are the Intune track:
their attributes come from device

96
00:06:19.555 --> 00:06:23.700
provisioning, never from HR, and a
device rule can't read its owner.

97
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:28.625
Servers are the Arc track, tagged at
onboarding and governed by Azure Policy.

98
00:06:28.625 --> 00:06:32.524
Non-person identities, like
service accounts and AI agents,

99
00:06:32.524 --> 00:06:34.513
answer to their owner's record.

100
00:06:34.513 --> 00:06:39.132
The goal is the same for all four:
true attributes, one source each,

101
00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:43.663
read by policy, so AD can retire
without losing what the tree did.

102
00:06:43.663 --> 00:06:48.030
Group Policy splits the same
way, so policy is next.

103
00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:56.028
Group Policy was Active Directory's
most ambitious delivery system,

104
00:06:56.028 --> 00:06:57.668
and its most network-bound.

105
00:06:57.668 --> 00:07:00.918
A client only gets it when it
can reach a domain controller.

106
00:07:00.918 --> 00:07:06.205
A cloud-native device, Entra joined
and in Intune, has no such dependency.

107
00:07:06.205 --> 00:07:07.730
Don't copy it across.

108
00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:11.931
A fifty-setting GPO ported as
fifty settings carries dead weight,

109
00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:17.455
old conflicts and manual targeting, and
Microsoft itself recommends starting fresh.

110
00:07:17.455 --> 00:07:22.727
So the program regenerates one clean
policy per category, keyed to attributes,

111
00:07:22.727 --> 00:07:25.971
into Intune, Conditional
Access and, for servers,

112
00:07:25.971 --> 00:07:31.080
Azure machine configuration, because
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

113
00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.373
Every change passes four
stages: validation,

114
00:07:34.373 --> 00:07:38.468
a simulated tenant, a report-only
pilot, then production.

115
00:07:38.468 --> 00:07:40.390
And prefer require over block,

116
00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:44.004
except where the federal SCuBA
baseline demands a block.

117
00:07:44.004 --> 00:07:47.930
Books 05 and 04 carry the detail.

118
00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.464
DNS, DHCP and address
management are what agencies

119
00:07:55.464 --> 00:07:58.618
most often put off,
because they still work.

120
00:07:58.618 --> 00:08:01.755
That's exactly how they keep
the old directory alive.

121
00:08:01.755 --> 00:08:05.395
AD-integrated zones replicate
with Active Directory,

122
00:08:05.395 --> 00:08:10.814
and domain-joined clients find their
domain controllers through records in DNS,

123
00:08:10.814 --> 00:08:14.292
so DNS and AD have to be
retired together, in sequence.

124
00:08:14.292 --> 00:08:17.982
The target is a governed
DNS authority outside AD.

125
00:08:17.982 --> 00:08:21.581
Windows DHCP servers check
in with AD every hour,

126
00:08:21.581 --> 00:08:26.080
but that only stops a rogue
Windows server, not a rogue device.

127
00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.606
And one address-management
authority owns the address space,

128
00:08:30.606 --> 00:08:33.180
with DNS and DHCP following from it.

129
00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:37.068
Leave these three for “Phase
2,” and it rarely comes.

130
00:08:37.068 --> 00:08:41.043
A forest that still hosts
DNS hasn't been retired.

131
00:08:41.043 --> 00:08:44.630
Books 08 and 09 carry the detail.

132
00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.435
Once identity, policy
and services have moved,

133
00:08:51.435 --> 00:08:55.455
the biggest remaining dependency
is the domain join itself.

134
00:08:55.455 --> 00:08:58.942
User PCs end up Entra
joined and in Intune.

135
00:08:58.942 --> 00:09:02.368
Servers come under Azure Arc
and machine configuration.

136
00:09:02.368 --> 00:09:05.530
Hybrid join is a waypoint,
not a destination.

137
00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:09.185
And there's no supported way to
convert a hybrid-joined device

138
00:09:09.185 --> 00:09:13.055
without resetting Windows, so time
the move to a hardware refresh.

139
00:09:13.055 --> 00:09:17.938
Every service principal name is an
application that expects Kerberos,

140
00:09:17.938 --> 00:09:21.993
and each needs a decision: move
it to modern authentication,

141
00:09:21.993 --> 00:09:25.717
retire it, or, as a last resort,
isolate it with an end date.

142
00:09:25.717 --> 00:09:29.834
No server leaves the domain
until every one is resolved,

143
00:09:29.834 --> 00:09:31.981
and NTLM goes in the same pass.

144
00:09:31.981 --> 00:09:35.855
And compute stays with its data
until the round trips are cut down.

145
00:09:35.855 --> 00:09:39.410
Books 05 and 06 have the detail.

146
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.235
When Active Directory retires, being
inside the network stops meaning anything.

147
00:09:48.235 --> 00:09:54.173
M-22-09 puts it simply: users should
log into applications, not networks.

148
00:09:54.173 --> 00:09:59.813
The replacement has four parts: Zero Trust
as the model, Conditional Access as the

149
00:09:59.813 --> 00:10:05.285
per-request decision, SASE at the edge,
and phishing-resistant MFA as the proof.

150
00:10:05.285 --> 00:10:10.447
BOD 25-01 makes blocking legacy
authentication mandatory.

151
00:10:10.447 --> 00:10:11.635
Smart cards stay.

152
00:10:11.635 --> 00:10:16.677
Entra certificate-based authentication
moves card validation from the domain

153
00:10:16.677 --> 00:10:19.687
controller to Entra ID,
though a card only counts

154
00:10:19.687 --> 00:10:22.848
as phishing-resistant if
it's set up as multifactor.

155
00:10:22.848 --> 00:10:28.162
And card issuance needs a successor first,
because an enterprise CA depends on AD.

156
00:10:28.162 --> 00:10:31.097
Administrators hold no standing privilege:

157
00:10:31.097 --> 00:10:35.222
roles are activated just in time,
and a vault covers what can't be.

158
00:10:35.222 --> 00:10:40.910
Book 04 covers policy and privilege,
and Book 07 the certificates.

159
00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:45.523
Who leads it?

160
00:10:45.523 --> 00:10:47.700
Chapter 09 sums it up in one line:

161
00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:52.809
Microsoft provides the instruments;
the program provides the orchestra.

162
00:10:52.809 --> 00:10:54.494
The tools are each excellent,

163
00:10:54.494 --> 00:10:58.776
but none of them decides what the
organization's structure should become,

164
00:10:58.776 --> 00:11:03.197
or what to do with what they find, and
no vendor deadline forces that decision.

165
00:11:03.197 --> 00:11:06.009
So name one decider for
each kind of decision.

166
00:11:06.009 --> 00:11:12.322
The sponsor and CIO own scope, funding,
sequence, and when AD components retire.

167
00:11:12.322 --> 00:11:15.871
The authorizing official is the
only one who can accept risk,

168
00:11:15.871 --> 00:11:18.285
so every deferred cutoff lands with them.

169
00:11:18.285 --> 00:11:20.683
The CISO sets control requirements,

170
00:11:20.683 --> 00:11:24.473
and the steward holds the
repository and clears most gates.

171
00:11:24.473 --> 00:11:28.316
Then, each month, ask three
things: how drift is trending,

172
00:11:28.316 --> 00:11:33.130
whether changes are signed, and
what risk acceptances are open.

173
00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:37.905
How do you get there?

174
00:11:37.905 --> 00:11:39.443
Start with an inventory.

175
00:11:39.443 --> 00:11:43.537
You can't modernize what you haven't
counted, and nothing gets retired

176
00:11:43.537 --> 00:11:47.130
until it's written down: read-only,
with proof of how it was read.

177
00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.841
The assessment account
has no write rights,

178
00:11:49.841 --> 00:11:53.530
the code can't issue write
commands, and every call is logged.

179
00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:58.631
An ordinary signed-in account already
sees more than most teams expect:

180
00:11:58.631 --> 00:12:02.868
the topology, the OU tree, most
Group Policy, users and groups.

181
00:12:02.868 --> 00:12:08.293
Every run records who, when, against
what, and a hash of every file.

182
00:12:08.293 --> 00:12:10.680
Eleven streams feed the risk scoring.

183
00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:16.395
One example: Windows Server 2016
reaches end of support on January 12,

184
00:12:16.395 --> 00:12:20.205
2027, so every such server
needs a disposition.

185
00:12:20.205 --> 00:12:24.790
And no application leaves its data
until its transactions are profiled.

186
00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:33.027
Chapter 11 turns the inventory
into change, in three stages.

187
00:12:33.027 --> 00:12:37.077
Analyze compares the current state
with the target and produces a diff.

188
00:12:37.077 --> 00:12:41.171
Plan expands that diff into
actions, each scoped to one object,

189
00:12:41.171 --> 00:12:44.703
put in dependency order, and
paired with its rollback.

190
00:12:44.703 --> 00:12:49.040
A plan has to pass validation, then
approval by someone who didn't write it.

191
00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:53.478
Then a separate identity carries it
out and closes with an evidence packet.

192
00:12:53.478 --> 00:12:58.110
How far automation may go is capped
by a ladder, from simply recording,

193
00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.441
up to autonomy for a short
list of low-risk changes.

194
00:13:01.441 --> 00:13:03.941
The default ceiling is gated actuation,

195
00:13:03.941 --> 00:13:07.427
because it's a posture an
authorizing official can sign.

196
00:13:07.427 --> 00:13:10.586
CISA calls its maturity
model a general guide,

197
00:13:10.586 --> 00:13:14.715
not a strict set of requirements,
so this is design, not mandate.

198
00:13:14.715 --> 00:13:19.190
And when nothing has drifted,
the next run plans zero actions.

199
00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:27.657
Chapter 12 puts it all in sequence: an
illustrative fifty-two-week program,

200
00:13:27.657 --> 00:13:32.762
seven phases from pre-flight to steady
state, with five workstreams in parallel.

201
00:13:32.762 --> 00:13:35.914
Large federal estates
usually take several years;

202
00:13:35.914 --> 00:13:38.752
the weeks are a sequencing
model, not a promise.

203
00:13:38.752 --> 00:13:43.335
A phase opens only when the previous
gate clears, and gates read from data.

204
00:13:43.335 --> 00:13:47.281
“Zero drift for fourteen days”
is a query, not a signature.

205
00:13:47.281 --> 00:13:52.191
Underneath it all is one structural
move: separate truth from enforcement.

206
00:13:52.191 --> 00:13:56.139
A governance layer holds and
reconciles the authoritative state,

207
00:13:56.139 --> 00:14:00.274
while the identity, network and device
platforms enforce it at runtime.

208
00:14:00.274 --> 00:14:01.996
And read one clock carefully:

209
00:14:01.996 --> 00:14:06.008
FedRAMP's 2027 date is about
certifying cloud providers.

210
00:14:06.008 --> 00:14:09.054
The agency still authorizes
its own systems.

211
00:14:09.054 --> 00:14:11.840
That brings us to the decisions.

212
00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:17.035
So, four decisions.

213
00:14:17.035 --> 00:14:21.535
First, name the owners, one decider per
decision, starting with the sponsor,

214
00:14:21.535 --> 00:14:24.116
the authorizing official and the steward,

215
00:14:24.116 --> 00:14:28.985
and confirm who holds the Zero Trust
lead role that M-22-09 required.

216
00:14:28.985 --> 00:14:34.647
Second, run the read-only assessment across
all eleven categories, with provenance.

217
00:14:34.647 --> 00:14:39.885
Third, stand up the platform host, so
every change has one writer and a plan.

218
00:14:39.885 --> 00:14:43.647
And fourth, set the dates: gate
dates on the sponsor's calendar,

219
00:14:43.647 --> 00:14:47.410
and every exception's end date
on the authorizing official's.

220
00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:50.048
None of this needs a vendor
decision on day one.

221
00:14:50.048 --> 00:14:53.135
Start with an inventory,
an owner, and a date.

222
00:14:53.135 --> 00:14:57.110
Modernization is a governance
rebuild, not a platform swap.

223
00:14:57.110 --> 00:15:02.425
Book 01 is the map, and Books 02
to 09 carry each part in depth,

224
00:15:02.425 --> 00:15:04.972
with every claim footnoted.

225
00:15:04.972 --> 00:15:05.790
Thank you.
